abstract="The rapid evolution of immersive technology has significantly expanded the potential for virtual reality to facilitate clinical communication training within medical and nursing education. To address the limitations of traditional role-playing, which often struggles to replicate the complex and resistant behaviors of pediatric patients, this study develops a generative-AI-driven VR health consultation training system. The system incorporates a trust-based interaction control mechanism, utilizing large language models to evaluate verbal strategies in real-time and dynamically adjust the virtual child's behavioral responses and trust levels. A multimodal learning analytics approach was employed to capture and analyze the spatial behaviors, physiological signals, and subjective evaluations of four pre-service school nurses across scenarios of varying children's personalities. Experimental results demonstrate that the system effectively simulates distinct scenarios, where the integration of these multimodal datasets reveals specific behavioral and physiological patterns that distinguish between effective and ineffective communication trajectories. This research validates the complementary value of such data in assessing complex consultation skills and provides an empirical foundation for the development of personalized, adaptive intelligent training platforms.",
isbn="978-3-032-31421-5"
}

